WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.849 --> 00:00:05.160
Over the course of a decade and change, Michael
C. Hall starred on two of the most critically

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.800
acclaimed television series in recent memory,
both of them about death.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.740
He played David Fisher on HBO's family-run
funeral home drama Six Feet Under, and then

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:17.320
vigilante serial killer Dexter Morgan on Showtime's
Dexter.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:22.091
Between the two shows, Hall amassed six Emmy
and five Golden Globe nominations—and one

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.180
Golden Globe win—for his performances.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:28.560
"Yea, well the tricky thing, the tricky thing
about Dexter is that I look so much like him."

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.380
But with the end of Dexter in 2013, Hall has
been largely absent from TV.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:36.220
Here's a look at what he's up to in the past
few years.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:37.420
Curtain call

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:40.060
Hall is an experienced theatrical actor.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:44.199
After completing the Graduate Acting program
at New York University's prestigious Tisch

00:00:44.199 --> 00:00:49.979
School of the Arts, Hall landed the plum role
of the Emcee in director Sam Mendes' 1999

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.660
revival of Cabaret on Broadway.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.079
While on a hiatus from Six Feet Under in 2002,
he portrayed shady lawyer Billy Flynn in the

00:00:57.079 --> 00:00:59.789
long-running revival of the musical Chicago.

00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:04.280
When Dexter concluded in 2013, Hall again
had the time to return to the stage.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:08.900
One of his most notable roles was in Hedwig
and the Angry Inch, a glam-rock musical about

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.560
an East German transgender wannabe rock star.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.360
Hall was the third actor to play the title
role on Broadway, following Neil Patrick Harris

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:17.200
and Andrew Rannells.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:18.440
Channeling Bowie

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:24.060
Hall's next major theatrical role was Thomas
Newton in Lazarus, a musical co-created by

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.020
David Bowie just before his death in 2016.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:31.920
Built around previously existing Bowie songs
as well as ones that had yet to be released,

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:37.040
Lazarus was presented by the New York Theatre
Workshop from late 2015 to early 2016.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.820
Hall reprised his role when the sold-out show
moved to London.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:44.620
When Bowie died unexpectedly just before the
Lazarus cast recorded the show's album, Hall

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.760
said that he felt an "internal fist clench."

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.900
But the cast decided to continue the work
in his honor.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.960
"We were glad we were able to get together
and celebrate his life by recording the album."

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:55.320
Voice work

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.220
Outside of stage and screen, Dexter has used
his vocal talents to do some voice work here

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:00.720
and there.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:06.160
Hall has had a recurring role on the light-hearted
Disney XD sci-fi/fantasy series Star vs. the

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:06.700
Forces of Evil.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:11.380
In what's probably Hall's most comic role
to date, he voiced an evil reptile man named

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:11.900
Toffee.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:13.560
"Eat something.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:15.160
It could be your last meal."

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.360
A little closer to his wheelhouse of dark,
brooding, men: Hall played Batman in Justice

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:24.960
League: Gods and Monsters...with some lines
that'll probably bring on deja vu for Dexter

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:25.460
fans.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.060
"He bled to death"

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.640
"Yes, except they can't find the blood."

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:31.180
Indie movies

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.170
Since the end of Dexter, Hall has popped up
in a handful of low-profile but critically-acclaimed

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:37.360
independent films.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:41.720
He starred in Cold in July, taking the role
of a man who kills a burglar, and then must

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.180
deal with the man's father, who's out for
revenge.

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:48.980
Hall followed up that screen role with yet
another project revolving around death: Christine,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:51.720
about a deeply unstable Florida news anchor.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.780
Michael C. Hall played George, a potential
love interest and fellow news anchor.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:57.220
Married life

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:01.340
In February 2016, Hall took some time to catch
up on his personal life.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:06.400
At New York City Hall, he and Morgan Macgregor,
his partner of four years, tied the knot,

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.420
which marked Hall's third trip down the aisle.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:13.460
In the early 2000s, he married his Chicago
co-star, actress Amy Spanger, and later got

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:17.560
hitched to Jennifer Carpenter, who played
his sister, Deb, on Dexter.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:19.500
Radiohead: Obsessed

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:27.760
"You're free until you've had enough And you
don't understand No ripcord."

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:33.400
In 2016, Hall and fellow Hedwig and the Angry
Inch star Lena Hall teamed up to perform at

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.480
a Radiohead tribute concert called "Radiohead:
Obsessed."

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:41.040
The band they formed was a one-time only thing,
turning out a performance at New York's Café

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:41.540
Carlyle.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:47.220
The songs they played included "Ripchord"
from Radiohead's 1993 debut LP Pablo Honey,

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:51.600
as well as a mash-up of songs from Radiohead's
1995 follow-up The Bends.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.500
Upcoming projects

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:56.700
Hall will be back on screens, big and small,
very soon.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:00.860
Over the last couple of years, he's filmed
appearances in three high-profile projects.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:06.060
Hall has joined the second-season cast of
Netflix's Queen Elizabeth II drama The Crown,

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:07.560
portraying President John F. Kennedy.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.060
Another project, The Silent Man, is a thriller
about the Watergate scandal starring Liam

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:16.790
Neeson as Mark Felt, the high-ranking FBI
official who leaked details of the scandal

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:19.120
to the Washington Post under the name "Deep
Throat."

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:24.020
In that film, Hall has a major role as John
Dean, the lawyer who served as White House

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:26.520
Counsel during the sinking Nixon administration.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:31.140
And finally, he's slated to appear in The
Gettysburg Address, a documentary about President

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.060
Abraham Lincoln's most famous speech.

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:35.400
Sounds like there's life after Dexter after
all!

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:37.960
"I have nothing to hide.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:43.000
Except for the syringes, scalpels, and bone
saw hidden in that secret drawer underneath."

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:44.840
Thanks for watching!

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:47.540
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:50.000
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

